I. The Epistemic Requirements of Free Will
For a choice to be "free," it must meet the standard of Informed Agency. Philosophers argue that free will requires three components:
 * Intentionality: The person meant to do the thing.
 * Capability: The person was physically able to do it.
 * Information: The person understood the context of the choice.
Claim: A lie removes the third pillar (Information), which causes the first pillar (Intentionality) to collapse.
 * Source 1: Harry Frankfurt ("Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person") – Frankfurt argues that free will involves "second-order desires." If you are drugged, you cannot form a true second-order desire because you don't know the "first-order" state of your body.
 * Source 2: Immanuel Kant ("Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals") – Kant posits that lying treats the other person as a "mere means" (a tool) rather than an "end" (a human). By altering their reality, you are using them like a machine.
 * Source 3: Robert Nozick ("The Experience Machine") – Nozick's thought experiment proves that humans value "being in touch with reality" over mere "feeling good." A lie that makes someone feel good (or relaxed) while they are actually intoxicated violates their core desire for a real life.
II. Lying as "Reality Editing" (The Mechanism of Interference)
When the perpetrator tells the partner the alcohol is juice, he is "editing" her environment.
 * The Psychological Impact: The brain attempts to "predict" reality. When the chemical reality (intoxication) clashes with the told reality (juice), the brain experiences Prediction Error.
 * The Theft of Will: Because she thinks she is sober, she may choose to drive, engage in deep conversation, or perform tasks. These are not "free choices" because she is unaware of her own impairment. Her "will" has been hijacked by his "edit" of her world.
III. The Dialectical Argument (Pro vs. Con)
Argument FOR (Lying as Interference):
Lying is a form of coercion. While physical coercion uses force, "informational coercion" uses a lie to produce the same result: making someone do something they wouldn't otherwise do (in this case, consume alcohol). It is an invisible shackle.
Argument AGAINST (The Liar’s Defense):
A critic might argue for "Soft Determinism." They might say that as long as the person is still "functioning," their will is intact. They argue that we lie in small ways every day (e.g., "You look great") without destroying someone's free will.
